\section{Experimental design}
\label{sec:design}

\paragraph{Graphs and designs.}
Synthetic graphs have $n=3000$ units and target mean degree $d\in\{5,20,80\}$: a stochastic block model (SBM; blocks of 100, 30\% of edges between blocks), Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi, Barab\'asi--Albert, Watts--Strogatz and a degree-corrected SBM (log-normal propensities) \citep{holland1983stochastic,erdos1959random,barabasi1999emergence,watts1998collective,karrer2011stochastic}. The SBM is the core family, with an $n$ slice ($10^3,3\times10^3,10^4$). The two real graphs are socfb-Stanford3 \citep{traud2012social,rossi2015network} and ego-Facebook \citep{mcauley2012learning}, reduced to the giant component and thinned by independent edge removal to a target degree; thinning also changes clustering and the degree tail, so a degree effect on real graphs is not attributed to degree alone (ego-Facebook at $d=80$ is unthinned, mean degree 43.7). Assignment is Bernoulli($p$), $p\in\{0.1,0.25,0.5,0.75,0.9\}$, or graph-cluster randomisation with Louvain clusters \citep{blondel2008fast} at resolution $\gamma\in\{0.3,1,3\}$ or the planted SBM partition and cluster-level Bernoulli($p$) draws. Louvain at $\gamma=0.3$ returns one cluster on every synthetic graph, so cluster designs there use $\gamma=1,3$ and the planted partition.

\paragraph{Mechanisms and calibration.}
Outcomes are $Y_i=a_i+h_i(z)+\varepsilon_i$ with unit baselines $a_i\sim N(0,1)$ (in some cells correlated with log-degree or community) and $h_i=(1-s)z_i+s\,\psi_i(z)$, where the exposure response $\psi_i$ is, with $f_i$ the treated-neighbour fraction and $c_i$ the count: (a) $f_i$; (b) $c_i/\bar d$; (c) saturating, $(1-e^{-3f_i})/(1-e^{-3})$; (d) threshold, $\mathbf 1[f_i\ge0.6]$; (e) 30-step diffusion; (f) $\tfrac12(f_i+z_if_i)$, an own-treatment$\times$exposure interaction (Table~\ref{tab:mech}, Appendix~\ref{app:grid}); (g), a convex mirror of (c), is exploratory. Every mechanism is calibrated to $\gate=1$ with spillover share $s\in\{0.25,0.5,0.75\}$ (fine grid $0.1,\dots,0.9$ at the core cell); $\gate$ is computed exactly by evaluating $h$ at $\mathbf 1$ and $\mathbf 0$ on each graph draw. Errors are $(\hgate-\gate)/\gate$. Noise $\varepsilon_i$ is iid Gaussian with standard deviation $\sigma$ times the standard deviation of the noise-free outcome ($\sigma\in\{0.25,0.5,1\}$ planned; $\sigma\in\{2,4\}$ added after the freeze and labelled as such). Under Bernoulli assignment DM's bias is $-s\gate$ by construction.

\paragraph{Estimators and tiers.}
Comparisons are made within an information tier.
\emph{Tier G} (graph known, no mechanism parameter): DM, H\'ajek ($q=0.75,1$), the reporting regression and its ladder (quadratic and spline in $f$, $+$count, $+$two-hop, $+zf$).
\emph{Tier A} (data-adaptive; Bernoulli designs, 14 core and real-graph cells, 200 draws per graph): lasso \citep{tibshirani1996regression} and ridge \citep{hoerl1970ridge} on 15 exposure features, with the penalty chosen by graph-cluster-fold cross-validation and a held-out-corner loss in the spirit of \citet{han2023model}; random forest \citep{breiman2001random} and gradient boosting \citep{friedman2001greedy} from scikit-learn \citep{pedregosa2011scikit}; and a simplified version of the adaptive-threshold H\'ajek estimator of \citet{thiyageswaran2024data}, reported under that name; and a two-layer graph neural network \citep{kipf2017semi} (one architecture) on 13 of the 14 cells and 50 draws, as it takes hours per cell (Appendix~\ref{app:extra}).
\emph{Tier O} (oracle): the correct feature with the true parameter, or with the parameter fitted by profile least squares for (c), (d), (e).
Causal message passing \citep{shirani2024causal} needs panel outcomes, is not run; the cluster designs use the H\'ajek form of the exposure estimators; the unnormalised Horvitz--Thompson form is worse (Appendix~\ref{app:extra}).

\paragraph{Replication, metrics and inference.}
A \emph{cell} is one combination of graph, degree, assignment design, $p$, mechanism, spillover share and noise level. A synthetic cell is 10 graph draws $\times$ 300 assignments; a real graph is one graph with 3000 assignments. Baselines are fixed per graph, noise is drawn per assignment, and all estimators see the same draws.
Bias, SD and RMSE of the normalised error are computed pooled over the draws of a cell (graph draws times assignments, SD $=\sqrt{\mathrm{MSE}-\mathrm{bias}^2}$, so it includes the variation between graph draws), with 95\% intervals from a bootstrap over graph draws (delta method for one graph); the ratio to DM is the paired $\log(\mathrm{RMSE}_{\rm reg}/\mathrm{RMSE}_{\rm DM})$, and ``regression wins'' means its paired bootstrap interval lies below zero. The 95\% intervals are $\hgate\pm1.96\,\mathrm{SE}$ with the HC0 sandwich SE \citep{white1980heteroskedasticity} (for cluster designs a cluster bootstrap, $B=100$; a unit bootstrap on a subset of draws gives the interval for DM). The \emph{estimated spillover share} is $\hat s=1-\overline{\hat\beta_z}/\overline{\hgate}$ from the same regression.

\paragraph{Diagnostic specified before the runs.}
Part (i), for the variance, is the leverage $L$ of each specification on the ladder against degree-only predictors of log realised variance. Part (ii), for the bias, uses $S$, the root-mean-square over the quadratic, spline, $+$count and $+$two-hop specifications of the difference between their error and the reporting regression's on the same assignments, and the raw corner distance times a curvature estimated from the quadratic term. Both are judged by held-out $R^2$ against the best of a set of degree benchmarks (mean degree, $p(1-p)$, $np(1-p)/d$, the bootstrap SE, a cross-validation loss), holding out whole mechanisms or graph families, with a bootstrap over groups; the selection and the test use disjoint halves of the data. The \emph{discriminating set} D holds the cells where predictors can differ (heterogeneous degree, cluster designs, real graphs, misspecified rungs) and the remaining cells are \emph{ties}; D$^*$ is the part of D in which misfit of the specification supplies at least 20\% of the variance of the estimate, chosen on a separate half of the draws. The diagnostic counts as \emph{supported} only if four criteria fixed in advance are all met (Table~\ref{tab:accept}, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}).

